"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[9508],{9508:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"id":"smc-01","slug":"smc-01","trackId":"smc","order":1,"title":"Trading and the Order Book","subtitle":"Structure, Confirmation and Risk Limits","estimatedMinutes":10,"access":"free","quizCount":3,"blueprintCount":0,"trainerIds":["HUB-CALC-09"],"markdown":"## Module Outline\\n\\n1. What is trading?\\n2. What is crypto?\\n3. How will our work be organized?\\n\\n## 1. What Is Trading?\\n\\nTrading means speculating in different assets to make a profit. We buy an asset or enter a trade to realize a profit when a target selected in advance through analysis is reached.\\n\\nSeveral types of trading can be distinguished (the boundaries are highly subjective):\\n\\n1. Short-term (from several hours to 3 days).\\n2. Medium-term (from 3 days to 3 months).\\n3. Long-term (from 3 months to an unlimited period).\\n\\n## Short-Term Trading\\n\\nThis is the main type of trading practiced by the MakeCash team. It is the most suitable form for full-time professional work. Short trades allow efficient use of time and provide quick feedback on a trading strategy.\\n\\nTechnical analysis is the main tool for trading decisions.\\n\\nTechnical analysis is a type of market analysis that uses information about price movements on a chart.\\n\\nMany different approaches work with this information. We use Smart Money and Price Action concepts.\\n\\nFutures trading is best suited to short-term trading.\\n\\n## Medium-Term Trading\\n\\nThis type of trading takes place over a longer period. It is used to trade short-term trends and narratives.\\n\\nTrading decisions use both technical and fundamental analysis.\\n\\nFundamental analysis is a type of market analysis that uses information about a project, sector and the overall market to develop trading hypotheses.\\n\\nThe longer the price-analysis timeframe, the more unpredictable variables appear that technical analysis cannot handle alone.\\n\\nSpot trading is best suited to medium-term trading, although futures can also be used.\\n\\n## Long-Term Trading\\n\\nThis type of trading has the longest horizon. It is used for long-term trends and narratives, and for fundamentally strong projects that influence the entire industry.\\n\\nTrading decisions rely mainly on fundamental analysis, with technical analysis used to refine trade entries and exits.\\n\\nHere, the time horizons are so long that technical analysis cannot provide all the information required. Fundamental indicators often point to prices far beyond those visible in the chart\'s history.\\n\\nSpot trading is used for long-term positions.\\n\\n## 2. What Is Crypto?\\n\\nThe word “crypto” refers to the entire cryptocurrency market. Many people think speculation is the only activity available, but the crypto market\'s technical characteristics open up many fields of work.\\n\\n- Token sales (ICO/IDO). You act as an investor who wants to fund a project you consider promising and profit from subsequent price growth when it launches.\\n- Airdrops. Activities on a project\'s platforms, such as likes, registrations, reposts and many others. These increase the project\'s marketing reach. Projects reward participants with tokens or stablecoins.\\n- Retroactive airdrops. Carrying out transactions within a service\'s network. This increases the project\'s transaction volumes and makes it more attractive to the market. A project may reward participants with an airdrop of its token.\\n\\n\xb7\\n\\n- Nodes. Technical support for network operations. You can help a project that is just launching. The team tests its code for errors and the blockchain\'s performance under load. A test network is launched, and you can operate one of its nodes. The project rewards this with tokens, sale access and so on.\\n\\n\xb7\\n\\n- You can also support a main network by becoming a miner (PoW networks) or validator (PoS networks). The blockchain automatically credits tokens for verifying blocks.\\n\\n\xb7\\n\\n- Ambassador programs. Helping develop a project\'s community through moderation, content work, translation and writing technical or organizational instructions. The project rewards this help with its assets or priority access to a token sale.\\n- Testnets. Testing services. Feedback helps developers check and improve their product. A project may reward participants with tokens, NFTs or priority access to a sale after the mainnet launch.\\n- NFTs. You can participate in a mint (similar to a sale: new NFTs from a collection are issued in exchange for your funds on the collection\'s website on mint day).\\n- You can speculate in NFTs or invest in them to profit from resale.\\n- DeFi (Decentralized Finance). A financial system similar to banking, but decentralized through blockchain. Competent allocation through DeFi tools — staking, farming and lending — can generate meaningful income.\\n- P2E (Play-to-Earn). You play games, create in-game activity and receive rewards from the project. Many branches of this model now exist, including M2E (Move-to-Earn), L2E (Learn-to-Earn) and others.\\n\\nAll these fields can be highly profitable, but depend heavily on market conditions. Trading is much more stable in that respect.\\n\\nThis course does not cover work in those areas. However, the fundamental-analysis topics provide a basis for selecting projects, including for other crypto activities. This helps filter out weak products and focus on better ones.\\n\\n## 3. How Will Our Work Be Organized?\\n\\n## Schedule\\n\\nThe school operates Monday through Saturday, from 9:00 to 23:00 Kyiv time. Sunday is a day off.\\n\\n## Work Platforms\\n\\nLearning involves three elements: the learning platform, an assistant bot and a Telegram work chat.\\n\\n## Learning Platform\\n\\nThe main learning activities take place on the MakeCash learning platform, to which you have already received access.\\n\\nStudy materials, educational videos, lecture streams, quizzes, homework submission and assessment all take place on the MakeCash learning platform.\\n\\n## Assistant Bot\\n\\nTo access the work chat, link your Telegram account to the learning platform through the assistant bot. Linking and chat-access instructions are in the welcome email. The assistant bot also sends important announcements and homework-assessment notifications.\\n\\nWork Chat\\n\\nYou can ask any questions in the Telegram work chat. It is designed to provide answers as quickly as possible. Mentors are on duty in the chat from 9:00 to 23:00.\\n\\nA sincere request: before asking a question in the work chat, try using search. Your question has probably been answered before. We ask this not because answering is difficult, but to avoid large amounts of duplicate information. This is primarily in your interests.\\n\\nIf you cannot find an answer, formulate your question carefully before posting it. This makes the mentor\'s work easier and helps them give the best possible answer.\\n\\n## Learning Process\\n\\nLearning follows this sequence:\\n\\n1. Study the written materials.\\n2. Watch the video lesson.\\n3. Watch the Q&A session/lecture stream.\\n4. Complete the quiz.\\n5. Submit the homework.\\n\\nAccording to the schedule on the learning platform\'s home page, access to a new lesson opens at 06:00 Kyiv time. It contains a video lesson and study materials. If the topic includes a Q&A session/lecture stream, it takes place that evening.\\n\\nThe main video lessons are provided in Beginner, usually accompanied by Q&A sessions.\\n\\nAdvanced and Proficiency mainly use lecture streams that combine a video lesson and a Q&A session. For simplicity, we will refer to both Q&A sessions and lecture streams as streams.\\n\\nStreams usually take place between 18:00 and 20:00 Kyiv time. The exact time is announced in advance through the Telegram bot (usually around 13:00). They last 1.5–2 hours.\\n\\nEvery stream is recorded and uploaded the next morning. If you are late or cannot attend a learning stream, you can watch the recording and ask any questions in the Telegram work chat.\\n\\nIf a module has no video lesson or written study materials, the main emphasis is on the learning stream.\\n\\nHomework becomes available with the lesson. It has two parts: a quiz and practical work.\\n\\n## Quizzes\\n\\nYou cannot proceed to the practical assignment until you pass the quiz. Some modules have practical work without a quiz, and others have a quiz without practical work.\\n\\nImportant: to select the correct answer, tick the checkbox rather than merely clicking the answer\'s surrounding area.\\n\\nWe advise against submitting homework immediately; prepare first. Without preparation, you will not pass the quiz, and even if you do, the mentor will not accept the practical assignment.\\n\\n## Practical Assignment\\n\\nThe practical assignment is a list of tasks to complete in order, following the “Homework Examples,” then send to a mentor for assessment. All this takes place on the MakeCash learning platform.\\n\\nMentors mainly assess practical assignments from 09:00 to 12:00 and 18:00–22:00. When possible, they also assess work between these periods.\\n\\n## Leaderboard\\n\\nYou receive points for completing quizzes and practical assignments. These points are added together and entered in a leaderboard or student ranking. Each package has its own leaderboard, with point accumulation ending at the end of the purchased package.\\n\\nFor example, Proficiency students accumulate points from Beginner, Advanced and Proficiency. Advanced students accumulate points from Beginner and Advanced. Beginner students accumulate points only from Beginner.\\n\\nThe top 5 students registered on WhiteBIT through the MakeCash referral link and having completed KYC receive gifts from the MakeCash team. The link and detailed instructions are in the “Working With an Exchange” lesson. That is another reason to make an effort and submit the best assignment you can.\\n\\nFor quizzes: each correct answer = 1 point. If you answer fewer than 70% of questions correctly, you must retake the quiz (7 correct answers out of 10 questions and 3 correct answers out of 5 questions). Each retake costs −1 point.\\n\\nFor example, a quiz contains 10 questions. You answer 6 correctly, which is below 70%. The system will not allow you to proceed to the practical assignment until you pass. You pass on the 3rd attempt and answer all 10 correctly. However, the 2 previous failed attempts subtract 2 points from your 10 points. You therefore receive 8 points, which are combined with the practical assignment\'s points and added to the leaderboard.\\n\\nQuiz completion time is also recorded. This introduces a differentiating factor into the results and simplifies leaderboard calculation when two or more students have equal points.\\n\\nFor practical assignments: your grade equals the number of points awarded by the mentor. A grade of 5 therefore adds 5 points to the leaderboard.\\n\\nThere are rules here too. If you rush to submit a practical assignment, you will probably make mistakes. The mentor will correct them and ask you to redo the work with those corrections in mind. Your final grade will then be 4 rather than 5. If you repeat mistakes even after the mentor\'s corrections and a discussion of what you did not understand, the grade will decrease further.\\n\\n---","blueprints":[],"quiz":[{"id":"q-smc-01-1","prompt":"Which trade holding period is classified as short-term trading in this course\'s methodology?","options":[{"id":"smc-01-1-1","label":"From several hours to 3 days","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. From several hours to 3 days"},{"id":"smc-01-1-2","label":"From 3 months to 5 years","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. From several hours to 3 days"},{"id":"smc-01-1-3","label":"Only trades lasting less than 5 seconds","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. From several hours to 3 days"}],"explanation":"From several hours to 3 days","sourceRef":"SMC_FREE:19–28"},{"id":"q-smc-01-2","prompt":"What does an exchange\'s Order Book show?","options":[{"id":"smc-01-2-1","label":"Current buy (Bid) and sell (Ask) limit orders at specific price levels","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. Current buy (Bid) and sell (Ask) limit orders at specific price levels"},{"id":"smc-01-2-2","label":"A guaranteed price forecast one month ahead","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Current buy (Bid) and sell (Ask) limit orders at specific price levels"},{"id":"smc-01-2-3","label":"Only completed trades from last year","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Current buy (Bid) and sell (Ask) limit orders at specific price levels"}],"explanation":"Current buy (Bid) and sell (Ask) limit orders at specific price levels","sourceRef":"SMC_FREE:210–255"},{"id":"q-smc-01-3","prompt":"What is the main advantage of trading short-term structure with a fixed stop-loss?","options":[{"id":"smc-01-3-1","label":"A complete absence of losing trades","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Quick feedback from the trading strategy and strict risk control for each trade"},{"id":"smc-01-3-2","label":"Quick feedback from the trading strategy and strict risk control for each trade","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. Quick feedback from the trading strategy and strict risk control for each trade"},{"id":"smc-01-3-3","label":"The ability to trade without risk-management rules","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Quick feedback from the trading strategy and strict risk control for each trade"}],"explanation":"Quick feedback from the trading strategy and strict risk control for each trade","sourceRef":"SMC_FREE:25–35, 5004–5018"}],"source":{"file":"01_FREE_GIFT_MakeCash_SMC_Sniper_Base_7_Modules.md","sha256":"f057cfd88e5ba88e2af764e9a8bf1d0d1b60fb53d3c11a314f7a56f680a6e4e5","lineStart":7,"lineEnd":175}}')}}]);